// Deal or Force - timed income penalties (IncomePenalties.cs).
//
// A consequence - a crisis, a disaster, a sanction, a historical shock - no longer takes a lump
// out of the treasury. It takes a share of the country's GROSS budget revenue (revenue before
// any expense) for a number of days. Expenses are untouched: a government that keeps spending
// through it borrows or cuts. It hurts the economy that earns, not whoever happened to save.
//
// Content writes it as  "income_penalty": { "share": 0.08, "days": 120 }.
//
// THE CONVERSION RULE (how an old one-off loss became a share and a length):
//   expected loss = share x gross daily revenue x days, and gross revenue in this model is about
//   six per cent of GDP a year (measured on the ten-year soak: 4.8 % at the start, 10 % a decade
//   on). Event files charged a share t of the TREASURY, and a treasury runs about five per cent
//   of GDP across a campaign (ten at the start, two to six later), so
//       share x days  ~=  t x 0.05 x 364 / 0.06  ~=  300 x t
//   e.g. treasury_percent -0.02 became share 0.05 for 120 days (6.0 ~= 300 x 0.02).
//   Hard-coded crises charged a share of GDP; they convert at run time against the country's own
//   last gross week (IncomePenalties.FromLoss), so the expected loss matches the old lump for
//   that country, held at IncomePenaltySingleMax and stretched in time instead, up to
//   IncomePenaltyMaxDays; anything beyond three years is forgiven rather than charged at once.
{
  // All penalties together never take more than half a week's gross revenue: a country under
  // three disasters and a crisis still collects something, and the cap is what keeps stacking
  // from becoming a death spiral into default.
  "IncomePenaltyCap": 0.5,
  // One converted shock never takes more than a quarter; a bigger bill runs longer instead.
  "IncomePenaltySingleMax": 0.25,
  // A converted penalty runs at least four weeks (shorter is a lump with extra steps) and at most
  // three years (longer outlives the government that caused it).
  "IncomePenaltyMinDays": 28,
  "IncomePenaltyMaxDays": 1095,
  // Penalties one country carries at once; a further one joins the longest-running entry.
  "IncomePenaltyMaxEntries": 16,
  // Gross revenue as a share of GDP for a country whose first budget week has not closed.
  "IncomePenaltyReferenceRevenueShare": 0.06,
  // Over how many days the hard-coded crises spread their cost: a financial crisis over its
  // slump (CrisisRecessionDays), a pandemic over its year, a disaster over the half-year it takes
  // to reopen what it wrecked.
  "CrisisIncomePenaltyDays": 540,
  "PandemicIncomePenaltyDays": 365,
  "DisasterIncomePenaltyDays": 180
}
